.TH MU2E-POWER-ON 1 "2026-09-18" "mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.1.0" "Mu2e DAQ Operations"
.SH NAME
mu2e-power-on \- phase 2: bring the Mu2e DAQ cluster up in dependency order
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mu2e-power-on
.RB [ \-\-execute ]
.RB [ \-\-from
.IR STAGE ]
.RB [ \-\-until
.IR STAGE ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Runs phase 2 of
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1):
the power-on sequence defined in
.BR mu2edaq-power-sequence.yaml (5).
.PP
For each stage, in order:
.IP \(bu 2
read each node's chassis power state over IPMI;
.IP \(bu 2
issue
.B chassis power on
to whatever is off;
.IP \(bu 2
wait for SSH to answer, up to the stage's
.IR boot_timeout ;
every node of the stage is waited for at once, under one stage deadline, so a
stage whose nodes never answer costs one
.IR boot_timeout ,
not one per node;
.IP \(bu 2
wait the stage's
.I settle
period, so late-starting units are not sampled mid-start;
.IP \(bu 2
run the stage's check profile;
.IP \(bu 2
decide whether the stage met its
.I require
setting
.RB ( all ", " majority " or " any ).
.PP
The shipped order is gateways, then
.IR mu2e-mgr-01 ,
then the data loggers, the DCS servers, the CFO, and finally the detector
readout nodes. It is operational rather than arbitrary:
.I mu2e-mgr-01
exports
.I /home
and the shared areas, so every later node's mounts fail if it is not healthy
first; the CFO distributes the clock and control fabric the readout nodes
synchronise to.
.PP
A stage that does not meet its requirement stops the sequence, unless
.B \-\-continue\-on\-error
is given. That is deliberate: later stages depend on the services the earlier
ones provide, so continuing would produce failures that say nothing new.
.SH SCOPE AND DEPENDENCIES
What the phase may touch is decided before any credential is acquired, from
.BR \-\-from / \-\-until ,
.B \-\-location
and
.BR \-\-node .
A selection that cannot be honoured exactly is an error (exit 2) \(em never a
silent widening.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-from
and
.B \-\-until
must name stages exactly; an unknown name, a reversed range, or a sequence with
duplicate stage names is rejected with the list of valid names.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-location
drops stages whose
.I location:
is not among those requested, with a notice; if no stage is left, that is an
error. The shipped sequence is all MC-2, so
.B \-\-location teststand
alone has nothing to run.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-node
cuts the stages that hold the named nodes down to those nodes. The stages
before them, from the start of the
.BR \-\-from / \-\-until
range, are
.B predecessors
and are included
.BR verify-only :
their power state is read, they are waited for and checked, but no power command
is sent to them. If a predecessor stage is not up \(em a node in it is off,
never answers ssh, or the stage misses its
.I require
setting \(em the run stops before the requested nodes' stage, whatever
.B \-\-continue\-on\-error
says, and tells you to run that stage explicitly
.RB ( "\-\-from STAGE \-\-until STAGE" ).
Stages after the last one holding a requested node are not run (SKIP, "outside
requested scope").
.B \-\-from
bounds how far back predecessors reach:
.B "\-\-from readout \-\-node mu2e-trk-01"
touches that one node and nothing else.
.IP \(bu 2
A
.B \-\-node
name that is in no stage, or only in stages outside the range or the locations,
is an error naming the stage(s) it is in.
.PP
The notices describing all of this are printed before the run starts and are
kept in the report notes and the run store's events. Independently of the plan,
the power step refuses any host the plan did not allow, recording it as action
.IR out_of_scope .
.PP
Each BMC is driven through a gateway of its own location; a location whose
gateways do not answer has no IPMI there, rather than borrowing another site's.
.SH TIME LIMITS
.I run.phase_timeout
bounds the whole phase. It is checked before every stage and before each node's
power command, and every ssh call's timeout is capped at the time left, so the
phase overruns by at most the one call in flight. A stage's boot wait ends at
now +
.RI min( boot_timeout ", phase time left)."
Stages and nodes the budget never reached are UNKNOWN with
.IR "not run: phase_timeout expired" ,
distinct from a failed check and from an unreachable node; resume with
.BR \-\-from .
.SH SAFETY
This command is a
.B dry run
unless this invocation authorises live power commands, with
.B \-\-execute
or with the
.B MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_ARM
token (see
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1),
.BR "LIVE RUNS" ).
In a dry run power states are read, and the report shows what
.I would
have been switched on, but no chassis is touched.
.I run.dry_run: false
in the configuration alone does not arm anything; it is refused with exit 2.
.PP
.BR "chassis power off" ", " cycle " and " reset
are refused for the hosts listed under
.I protected:
in
.BR mu2edaq-topology.yaml (5)
\(em the gateways,
.I mu2e-mgr-01
and
.I mu2e-dcs-01
\(em regardless of
.BR \-\-execute .
The gateway stage never issues a power command at all; it verifies only.
.PP
All the BMCs share one credential set, so a refused BMC credential is not
retried and not repeated: the first rejection stops the run issuing IPMI and
produces one diagnosis naming the username that was refused, instead of walking
the same wrong credential through every chassis and locking the account on
each. Only a RAKP failure or
.I "unauthorized name"
counts;
.I "Unable to establish IPMI v2 / RMCP+ session"
does not, because that is also what a chassis with no standby power says, and
after an outage that is the expected case. A node whose power state could not
be read because of that refusal is recorded as
.IR credentials_refused ,
distinct both from a BMC that did not answer and from the deliberate
protected-host refusal. Set
.I ipmi.stop_on_auth_failure: false
to override.
.PP
Every attempt, including every refusal, is recorded in the run store before the
command is issued, so a tool that dies mid-action still leaves evidence of what
it was doing.
.SH OPTIONS
Every option of
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1)
is accepted except
.BR \-\-phase ,
which is fixed to
.BR poweron .
.SS Sequence control
.TP
.BI \-\-from " STAGE"
Start at this stage. Use it to resume after fixing whatever stopped a previous
attempt.
.TP
.BI \-\-until " STAGE"
Stop after this stage. Useful for bringing up the infrastructure nodes and
pausing before the detector readout.
.TP
.B "Spelling, for both of the above"
A stage name must match a
.I name:
key in
.BR mu2edaq-power-sequence.yaml (5)
.RB ( gateways ", " manager ", " dataloggers ", " dcs ", " cfo ", " readout )
exactly.
.B "mu2e-power-on \-\-execute \-\-from manger"
exits 2 before any credential is acquired, listing the valid names; so does a
reversed range, and so does a typo in
.I run.from_stage
or
.IR run.until_stage .
.TP
.B \-\-continue\-on\-error
Do not stop at the first stage that fails its requirement.
.SS Run control
.TP
.BI \-\-label " TEXT"
Label for this run, shown in the report header and the logbook subject.
.TP
.B \-\-execute
Authorises this invocation to switch things on. Without it (or the
.B MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_ARM
token) nothing is issued.
.TP
.B \-\-simulate
Contact nothing at all; answer every command from the built-in script. It
overrides
.B \-\-execute
on the same command line, so a rehearsal cannot become a real power-on by
leaving a flag behind.
.TP
.BI \-\-location " NAME"
Repeatable. Stages whose
.I location:
is not listed are not run. See
.BR "SCOPE AND DEPENDENCIES" .
.TP
.BI \-\-node " HOST"
Repeatable. Only these nodes are powered; the stages before theirs are
verified, never powered, and later stages are not run. See
.BR "SCOPE AND DEPENDENCIES" .
.TP
.B \-\-include\-failed
Accepted; it governs phase 3 and has no effect here.
.SS Credentials
.TP
.BI \-\-principal " PRINCIPAL"
Kerberos principal for ordinary logins, used for the post-boot checks and for
reaching the gateway that runs
.BR ipmitool (1).
.TP
.BI \-\-root\-principal " PRINCIPAL"
Kerberos principal with root access on the nodes, for the checks that need a
root session.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-prompt
Never prompt for a password; fail with instructions instead.
.TP
.BI \-\-vault\-addr " URL"
HashiCorp Vault address. The BMC credentials come from it, so this phase cannot
run without either Vault or the configured file fallback.
.SS Report
.TP
.BI \-\-output\-dir " DIR"
Where the report pages are written. Default:
.IR html/ .
.TP
.B \-\-publish
Copy the report to
.I report.publish.target
afterwards.
.TP
.BI \-\-publish\-target " DEST"
Override the publication target. Implies
.BR \-\-publish .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-report
Do not write the HTML pages; console output only.
.TP
.B \-\-post\-ecl
Accepted; the logbook entry is written by phase 4. See
.BR mu2e-power-report (1).
.TP
.BI \-\-run\-id " N"
Accepted; only phase 4 reads it.
.SS General
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the option summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print
.I mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.1.0
and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Main configuration file. Default:
.IR config/power-recovery.yaml .
.TP
.BI \-\-env\-file " FILE"
Dotenv file. Default:
.IR config/.env .
.TP
.BI \-\-database\-url " URL"
SQLAlchemy URL for the run store.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-self\-update
Skip the phase-0 check for a newer revision.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-checks
Print every registered check and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-nodes
Print the node inventory and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the result as JSON on stdout instead of tables.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Debug logging, including the exact
.B ipmitool
invocation and one line per credential attempted.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Warnings and errors only.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every stage met its requirement.
.TP
.B 1
The sequence ran but a stage failed, or nodes failed their checks.
.TP
.B 2
The sequence could not start: no gateway, no BMC credentials, a stage or node
selection that cannot be honoured, or a configuration that asks for a live run
this invocation did not authorise.
.SH EXAMPLES
See what would happen, without doing it:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-power-on
.EE
.RE
.PP
Bring up the infrastructure only, then stop and look:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-power-on \-\-execute \-\-until cfo
.EE
.RE
.PP
Resume at the DCS stage after repairing it:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-power-on \-\-execute \-\-from dcs
.EE
.RE
.PP
Power on one readout node, verifying (not powering) everything it depends on:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-power-on \-\-execute \-\-node mu2e-trk-01
.EE
.RE
.PP
Arm an unattended run from its environment instead of the flag (the
configuration must set
.I run.dry_run: false
and
.IR "run.label: sept-2026" ):
.RS
.EX
MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_ARM=sept-2026 mu2e-power-on \-\-no\-prompt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Rehearse the sequence with nothing attached:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-power-on \-\-simulate \-v
.EE
.RE
